WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.402 --> 00:00:21.446
Having provided bread for my family, I began to think again about the wreck and all the valuables

2
00:00:21.546 --> 00:00:21.966
on board.

3
00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:25.489
Most of all I wanted to acquire the beautiful little sailboat.

4
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.632
But I knew I would need a lot of help to do so.

5
00:00:29.493 --> 00:00:33.176
I began to coax my wife to let me take all the boys except Franz.

6
00:00:34.136 --> 00:00:39.080
"I'll consent to this," she finally said, "but you must promise me one thing."

7
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.523
"Anything," I said eagerly.

8
00:00:41.543 --> 00:00:45.566
"You'll never spend another night on that dangerous wreck."

9
00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:47.027
"I promise."

10
00:00:48.228 --> 00:00:50.070
The three boys were delighted to go along.

11
00:00:53.973 --> 00:00:58.037
At Safety Bay we fastened on our life preservers and stepped into the tub boat.

12
00:00:58.837 --> 00:01:01.720
With the raft in tow, we steered straight for the wreck.

13
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.221
On board I directed the boys to collect some provisions and load the raft.

14
00:01:07.061 --> 00:01:11.862
Meanwhile I studied the sailboat parts, which were stowed in a small compartment in the hold.

15
00:01:12.603 --> 00:01:16.424
The space was so narrow that it would be impossible to put the pieces together there.

16
00:01:17.144 --> 00:01:21.105
But the individual parts were so heavy that we couldn't move them to a bigger space.

17
00:01:21.865 --> 00:01:22.925
"Room to work in, boys!

18
00:01:23.286 --> 00:01:27.067
That's what we need first," I said when they came to see what I was doing.

19
00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:30.868
"Fetch axes and let's break down the walls around the sailboat."

20
00:01:31.608 --> 00:01:35.483
We worked hard, but as evening approached, we'd made little progress.

21
00:01:36.323 --> 00:01:38.365
Leaving the wreck, we headed for Safety Bay.

22
00:01:39.166 --> 00:01:43.250
Imagine our surprise when we found my wife and Franz waiting for us at Tentholm.

23
00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.473
She had decided to live there while we worked on the wreck.

24
00:01:46.493 --> 00:01:51.557
"That way you'll live nearer your work, and I won't lose sight of you," she said.

25
00:01:52.718 --> 00:01:53.959
I was delighted by her plan.

26
00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:58.884
"We'll work with great speed, so you may soon return to your beloved Falconhurst."

27
00:02:06.508 --> 00:02:09.390
We spent our days clearing an open space around the sailboat.

28
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.011
Then we worked on putting the parts together.

29
00:02:12.712 --> 00:02:17.274
We started early and returned at night, always bringing valuable cargo from the old wreck.

30
00:02:18.655 --> 00:02:20.836
At last the sailboat was ready to be launched.

31
00:02:21.537 --> 00:02:24.538
But it was still imprisoned within massive wooden walls.

32
00:02:25.419 --> 00:02:29.881
We had been able to free the boat from its compartment, but we couldn't break through the ship's

33
00:02:29.921 --> 00:02:34.244
hull. Now it seemed that our work would end in failure and disappointment.

34
00:02:34.984 --> 00:02:37.967
I was almost in despair when an idea suddenly came to me.

35
00:02:38.787 --> 00:02:43.211
I took a large cannon, filled it with gunpowder, and added a long fuse.

36
00:02:44.332 --> 00:02:47.834
Then I placed the cannon so that it would blow out the side of the ship when it

37
00:02:47.894 --> 00:02:51.878
exploded. After the boys were in the tub boat, I lit the fuse.

38
00:02:52.678 --> 00:02:54.800
My heart was pounding as I hurried after them.

39
00:02:56.121 --> 00:02:59.964
We sailed back to Safety Bay, where we began unloading the day's haul.

40
00:03:00.845 --> 00:03:04.448
No one noticed my anxiety as I waited for the sound of the explosion.

41
00:03:05.168 --> 00:03:07.170
And then it came—a mighty roar!

42
00:03:07.991 --> 00:03:09.292
A grand burst of smoke!

43
00:03:10.233 --> 00:03:14.396
Terror-stricken, my wife and children looked first toward the sea and then at me.

44
00:03:14.416 --> 00:03:20.201
"Perhaps you left a light burning near some gunpowder," suggested my wife.

45
00:03:21.002 --> 00:03:22.823
"That's possible," I said quietly.

46
00:03:22.844 --> 00:03:24.325
"I'll go see what's happened.

47
00:03:25.045 --> 00:03:26.086
Will anyone come with me?"

48
00:03:27.047 --> 00:03:28.708
The boys sprang into the tub boat.

49
00:03:29.529 --> 00:03:32.512
The wreck looked the same from the side where we usually approached.

50
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.982
But then we pulled around to the other side.

51
00:03:35.562 --> 00:03:39.185
There was a huge hole in the side of the ship, and the decks were torn apart.

52
00:03:39.785 --> 00:03:41.066
"Hurrah!" I cried.

53
00:03:41.086 --> 00:03:42.567
"The sailboat is ours!"

54
00:03:43.388 --> 00:03:44.889
The boys looked at me in surprise.

55
00:03:44.909 --> 00:03:47.070
"You planned that explosion!"

56
00:03:47.431 --> 00:03:48.011
said Ernest.

57
00:03:48.031 --> 00:03:50.293
"Yes, I did," I admitted.

58
00:03:50.313 --> 00:03:52.414
"But let's not tell Mother or Franz.

59
00:03:53.255 --> 00:03:55.917
I want them to be surprised when we sail into the bay."

60
00:03:56.738 --> 00:03:59.560
I had put rollers beneath the sailboat before the explosion.

61
00:04:00.432 --> 00:04:04.195
So with levers and pulleys we were able to gently lower the boat into the water.

62
00:04:04.856 --> 00:04:09.220
The boys spent the next few days helping me rig the boat and mount the two little brass

63
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.064
cannons. Casting off from the wreck and spreading our sail, we began our voyage.

64
00:04:14.965 --> 00:04:17.107
The sailboat glided swiftly through the water.

65
00:04:17.987 --> 00:04:18.808
I stood at the helm.

66
00:04:19.689 --> 00:04:22.632
Ernest and Jack manned the cannons while Fritz gave the signal.

67
00:04:22.652 --> 00:04:24.853
"Fire!" he cried.

68
00:04:25.614 --> 00:04:27.596
Bang! Bang!

69
00:04:28.544 --> 00:04:32.407
The sound of the cannons echoed among the cliffs, followed by our happy shouts.

70
00:04:33.367 --> 00:04:35.529
My wife and Franz greeted us onshore.

71
00:04:36.229 --> 00:04:37.510
Fritz helped them to come aboard.

72
00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:42.353
"I don't know whether to scold you or praise you," said Mother breathlessly.

73
00:04:42.373 --> 00:04:46.176
"First I was startled by the sight of this strange boat.

74
00:04:46.616 --> 00:04:47.777
Then I heard the cannons.

75
00:04:48.737 --> 00:04:52.580
If I hadn't recognized your shouts—" "But do you like our sailboat?"

76
00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:53.641
interrupted Jack.

77
00:04:53.661 --> 00:04:58.284
"I do indeed, you dear, horrid, wonderful boys."

78
00:04:59.125 --> 00:05:00.452
My wife gave Jack a hug.

79
00:05:00.472 --> 00:05:03.246
"But don't you ever scare me like that again!"
